---
title: Modules and plugins
description: IModule is the extension point the Command, Query and Event modules themselves use — and the way to add your own.
---

Ergosfare's core knows about messages, handlers, interceptors and pipeline plans. It knows
nothing about *commands*, *queries* or *events*. Those three are **modules** built on the same
extension point you can use yourself.

That is not a metaphor. `AddCommandModule` is four lines:

```csharp
public static IModuleRegistry AddCommandModule(
    this IModuleRegistry moduleRegistry,
    Action<CommandModuleBuilder> builderAction)
{
    moduleRegistry.Register(new CommandModule(builderAction));
    return moduleRegistry;
}
```

## The contract

A module is anything implementing `IModule`:

```csharp
public interface IModule
{
    void Build(IModuleConfiguration configuration);
}
```

`Build` runs once, at registration time. It receives everything a module needs:

```csharp
public interface IModuleConfiguration
{
    IServiceCollection Services { get; }        // register your own services
    IMessageRegistry MessageRegistry { get; }   // register messages, handlers, interceptors
}
```

Here is the whole command module:

```csharp
internal class CommandModule : IModule
{
    private readonly Action<CommandModuleBuilder> _builder;

    public CommandModule(Action<CommandModuleBuilder> builder) => _builder = builder;

    public void Build(IModuleConfiguration configuration)
    {
        _builder(new CommandModuleBuilder(configuration.MessageRegistry));

        configuration.Services.TryAddScoped<ICommandMediator, CommandMediator>();
    }
}
```

Two responsibilities: run the user's registration lambda against a builder, and add the
module's own facade to DI. `TryAddScoped` rather than `AddScoped`, so registering a module
twice is harmless.

## Writing your own module

The case for a custom module is a *new message shape* — something that is not a command, a
query or an event, but wants the same pipeline, the same interceptor stages and the same
compile-time discovery.

```csharp
public sealed class SagaModule : IModule
{
    private readonly Action<SagaModuleBuilder> _builder;

    public SagaModule(Action<SagaModuleBuilder> builder) => _builder = builder;

    public void Build(IModuleConfiguration configuration)
    {
        _builder(new SagaModuleBuilder(configuration.MessageRegistry));

        configuration.Services.TryAddScoped<ISagaMediator, SagaMediator>();
    }
}

public static class SagaModuleRegistryExtensions
{
    public static IModuleRegistry AddSagaModule(
        this IModuleRegistry registry, Action<SagaModuleBuilder> builderAction)
    {
        registry.Register(new SagaModule(builderAction));
        return registry;
    }
}
```

Used exactly like the built-in modules:

```csharp
services.AddErgosfare(o => o
    .AddCommandModule(c => c.RegisterGenerated())
    .AddSagaModule(s => s.RegisterFromAssembly(typeof(MySaga).Assembly)));
```

{% aside type="caution" %}
The source generator only emits `RegisterGenerated()` for the three built-in module builders.
A custom module has to feed the registry itself — through explicit `Register<T>()` calls, its
own `RegisterFromAssembly`, or `RegisterDescriptors(...)` if you pre-compute descriptors
yourself.
{% /aside %}

## Module builders

Every builder implements the same three entry points:

```csharp
public interface IModuleBuilder
{
    IModuleBuilder Register<T>();
    IModuleBuilder Register(Type type);
    IModuleBuilder RegisterFromAssembly(Assembly assembly);
}
```

The concrete builders add module-specific overloads on top — `CommandModuleBuilder` returns
`CommandModuleBuilder` from each call so chains stay typed, and adds
`RegisterDescriptors(IEnumerable<IHandlerDescriptor>)`, which is what generated registration
uses to hand over pre-computed descriptors without reflection.

## Only take what you need

Because the modules are independent, an application that never publishes events never
registers the event module, never resolves `IEventMediator`, and never carries its assembly:

```csharp
services.AddErgosfare(o => o
    .AddCommandModule(c => c.RegisterGenerated()));   // that's it
```

The same applies at the package level — see
[Installation](/ergosfare.docs/preview/getting-started/installation).

## The core module

`AddCoreModule` registers messages, handlers and interceptors that are not tied to any of the
three built-in shapes. It is rarely needed directly; the built-in modules already configure
what the runtime requires.

Next: [Modules and extensions](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-components/modules-extensions) walks
through the shipped modules in detail.
